From 781a42328538a8d53846b468a1ac3d3b5249705d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 09:27:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(videos): take upload date and view count from YouTube,
 not from memory
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every video watch page states two numbers to Google, and both were wrong.

The upload date was hand-typed into videos.ts. 52 of the 84 entries had
drifted onto the same placeholder, 2026-06-11, so videos genuinely
published in 2024 were declaring themselves as uploaded in June 2026 —
off by as much as two years. Checked against YouTube, only two dates in
the file were exactly right; the rest were either that placeholder or a
timezone's worth out.

The view count was reconstructed by stripping the digits out of the
display string and multiplying by 1000. That reads "6.1K views" as 61,000
and "741 views" as 741,000 — a claim the channel cannot support. It
happened to land close for anything already formatted as a round "NK",
which is why it went unnoticed.

Both now come from the YouTube API on every render, alongside the views
and duration that were already hydrated. The stored date survives only as
a fallback for when the API cannot be reached, and the view count is
carried as a raw integer rather than parsed back out of a lossy string.
When there is no number to state, the property is omitted rather than
guessed.

Also folds /our-videos/eldeco-7-peaks-omicron-1 into
/our-videos/eldeco-7-peaks-residences-greater-noida-review. Both watch
pages were built on the same YouTube video, so the site was competing
with itself for "Eldeco 7 Peaks" and splitting the link equity; the
surviving page is the one carrying the right date and the current title.

